=== task: mcq medium
title: When is it slow?
prompt: When do a hash map's `get` and `put` become O(n)?
* When many keys land in the same bucket, for example because the hash function gives many keys the same number :: Right. Then one bucket holds most of the pairs, and searching it is O(n).
- When the map has a lot of buckets :: More buckets means shorter chains, which is faster.
- When the keys are strings rather than numbers :: Strings hash fine; what matters is how evenly the hashes spread.
- When the values are very large :: Values aren't hashed or compared during a lookup; only keys are.
hint: What does `get` have to search?
hint: How big can one bucket get?
right: A hash map is fast only while its buckets are short. A bad hash function, like the length of a word, piles everything into a few buckets: O(n).
wrong: `get` searches one bucket. If most keys collide into it, that search is as slow as searching a list: O(n).

